---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StageTrack from "../components/StageTrack.astro";
import { data } from "../lib/export";
import { STAGE_PATH, STATUS_LABELS, STATUS_MEANINGS } from "../lib/status";

const codes = Object.keys(STATUS_LABELS) as (keyof typeof STATUS_LABELS)[];
---

<Base
  title="How it works"
  description="How a claim's status is set from approved records, what each status means, and what this site does not say."
>
  <h1 class="title">How it works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Each page tracks one reported breakthrough and shows the records found since it was reported.
    This page explains how the status on a claim page is set and what it does not tell you.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar">How a status is set</h2>
  <p>
    A status is derived by fixed rules from the approved evidence events listed on the claim page.
    Where no trial is found, the rules also use the kind of study the first reports described.
    No model and no editor decides a status. Every status lists the events that produced it.
  </p>
  <p>The rules are applied in this order, and the first one that fits sets the status.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      If the paper the first news reports were about has a retraction notice, the status is
      “{STATUS_LABELS.retracted}”.
    </li>
    <li>If a regulator has recorded an approval, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.approved}”.</li>
    <li>
      Otherwise, trials that have not been terminated, withdrawn or marked as missing their primary
      endpoint are the ones that count. If any remain, the status follows the furthest one that has
      started: “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_3}”, “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_2}” or “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_1}”.
      If none has started, or none has a phase, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.trial_registered}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      If no trial remains and a person has marked a trial as missing its primary endpoint, with a
      source link, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.failed}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      If no trial remains and every registered trial was terminated or withdrawn, the status is
      “{STATUS_LABELS.terminated}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      With no trial found at all, the status depends on what the first reports described. If it was
      a study in people, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.no_trial_found}”. If it was laboratory or
      animal work, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.preclinical_only}”.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A claim that is still at a stage open to further evidence (“{STATUS_LABELS.preclinical_only}”
    through “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_3}”, and “{STATUS_LABELS.no_trial_found}”) can also carry the
    no-signal note, described below.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar">What each status means</h2>
  <dl class="meanings">
    {
      codes.map((code) => (
        <div>
          <dt>
            {STAGE_PATH.includes(code) && <StageTrack code={code} />}
            {STATUS_LABELS[code]}
          </dt>
          <dd>{STATUS_MEANINGS[code]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2 class="bar">No signal</h2>
  <p>
    A claim that has stayed at an open stage for several years, with no new approved record, shows a
    note such as “No signal for 5 years”. The note means that the most recent record is that old.
    The count starts from the date of that record, or from the date the claim was first reported
    when no record has been found.
  </p>
  <p>
    It says nothing about work that has not been published or registered, and it is not a status.
    A new record removes it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar">What this site never says</h2>
  <p class="plain">
    This site gives no verdict on a claim, a researcher, a company or a news outlet. It gives no
    medical advice and recommends no treatment. A registered clinical trial is not a treatment
    option.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar">Where the data comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Every record on a claim page links to the source that holds it. Records are shown as the
    registries and databases gave them. The summaries and the wording of statuses are the site’s.
    News articles are linked and summarised; their text is not copied.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      data.sources.map((source) => (
        <li>
          <a href={source.url}>{source.name}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 class="bar">An optional search of a new report</h2>
  <p>
    When you ask for a registry search, OpenAI reads the report's text to identify its treatment
    and condition. We search ClinicalTrials.gov and linked research through Europe PMC, then
    use OpenAI to compare the trial records. This is a live search of those research sources,
    not a general search of the web. Its answer is marked as automatic and unreviewed; it cannot
    create a claim page or change a status. The service keeps neither the report nor the answer.
    Read <a href="/privacy">what is sent and how the providers handle it</a> before searching.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar">What is counted</h2>
  <p class="plain">Cloudflare, which hosts this site, counts requests and errors to operate the service. We do not add analytics, set tracking cookies, or keep reader profiles. Request logging for our Worker is disabled.</p>

  <h2 class="bar">Report an error</h2>
  <p>Each claim page says how to report an error in it.</p>
</Base>
